            <ArticleTitle>Pain complaint as the first symptom of oral cancer: a descriptive study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>56-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To identify pain as the initial symptom of oral cancer patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Hospital charts of 1412 patients (1977 to 1998) with oral cancer were reviewed (238 female and 1174 male).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Pain was the initial complaint in 19.2% of the sample. Oral cancer (ratio 4:1) and initial pain (ratio 9:1; P= .001) was prevalent in men. There were 12 different complaints of pain: sore throat (37.6%), tongue pain (14.0%), mouth pain (12.9%); pain when swallowing (11.1%), dental pain (5.9%); earache (5.9%); pain in the palate (4.1%); burning mouth (3.3%); gingival pain (2.2%); pain when chewing (1.1%); neck pain (1.1%), and facial pain (0.7%). Pain was associated with advanced TNM staging and location of tumor in the tongue (P= .004) and the tongue/mouth floor (P&lt; .006).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There were 12 different descriptions of pain; pain was related to TNM staging in the tongue and the tongue/mouth floor. The data presented reinforce the suggestion that patients with orofacial pain need systematized evaluation and sometimes require an interdisciplinary approach.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
